 coordinates in interleaved x y order. It preserves the constructor array type or uses Float64Array when created with the from factory.", "delaunay.update()": "This method updates the triangulation after in-place edits to coords while avoiding expensive memory allocations. It is useful for iterative relaxation algorithms such as Lloyd relaxation.", "Performance": "This section compares benchmark timings against other JavaScript Delaunay libraries across uniform, gauss, grid, and degenerate distributions. Delaunator is fastest by a wide margin on both 100k and 1M point inputs.", "Papers": "This section lists the academic ideas behind the sweep-based triangulation algorithm. It cites three papers published between 2010 and 2013 on sweep-line, S-hull, and circle-sweep methods.", "Robustness": "This section states that Delaunator should produce valid output even on highly degenerate input. It relies on robust-predicates, a modern port of Shewchuk robust geometric predicates.", "Ports to other languages": "This section lists community ports of Delaunator to many other programming languages. It includes Rust, Go, C++, C#, Ruby, Python, Kotlin, Java, Stata, and Julia implementations."}, "concerns": [], "rationale": "I read README.md via read_file, identified all headed sections, excluded Example per instructions, and wrote a concise two-sentence summary for each remaining section keyed by its exact title."}

